content.thinking:
Now I understand the bug. In `updateTask`, the current implementation explicitly assigns `updates.X as any` for every field, even if the value is `undefined`. This causes fields not in the update to be set to `undefined`, erasing the existing data.

The fix is to only apply updates for fields that are actually provided. Let me write a clean implementation:

```typescript
updateTask(id: string, updates: UpdateTaskInput): Task {
    const existing = this.tasks.get(id);
    if (!existing) throw new Error(`Task not found: ${id}`);

    if (updates.title !== undefined) validateTitle(updates.title);
    if (updates.priority !== undefined) validatePriority(updates.priority);
    if (updates.status !== undefined) validateStatus(updates.status);
    if (updates.dueDate !== undefined) validateDueDate(updates.dueDate);

    const updated: Task = {
      ...existing,
      ...(updates.title !== undefined && { title: updates.title.trim() }),
      ...(updates.description !== undefined && { description: updates.description }),
      ...(updates.priority !== undefined && { priority: updates.priority }),
      ...(updates.status !== undefined && { status: updates.status }),
      ...(updates.dueDate !== undefined && { dueDate: updates.dueDate }),
      updatedAt: toISOTimestamp(new Date()),
    };
    this.tasks.set(id, updated);
    this.invalidateCache();
    return { ...updated };
}
```

Wait, looking at `createTask`, it does `title: input.title.trim()` and `description: input.description ?? ""`. Let me match this behavior:
- For title, should we trim? In createTask, the input title is trimmed. In updateTask, we may want to also trim. Looking at the validators, it just checks if not empty. Let me apply the same behavior as createTask for consistency.

Actually looking at the test `updateTask merges correctly without losing fields`, after update it expects `updated.title` to be "Updated". If we trim, that works fine. Let me trim too for consistency.

Let me writethe fix.

